Results
We recruited 72 participants, 43 UAS gardeners from
the three selected UAS and 29 controls. 60% of both
UAS gardeners and controls were female. The majority of UAS gardeners were aged 41–59 years (60%),
33% were C 60 years old, and 7% were B 40 years
old. The control group had a more even distribution of
ages, 36%, 39% and 25% for the same age groups,
respectively. Additional participant demographics are
presented in Table 1.
Human biological data
The control group’s BLL range was 0.7–2.9 lg dL-1
with geometric mean 1.43 lg dL-1. The UAS gardeners’ BLLs ranged from 0.6 to 11.4 lg dL-1. An
UAS gardener with a BLL of 11.4 lg dL-1 was
removed from the data analyses as follow-up indicated
that the raised level was linked to renovating leaded
windows in the week prior to testing. The individual’s
ZPP level was not elevated (at 2.5 lg g-1 Hb) and
supported the self-reported claim of the exposure
being an isolated recent occurrence. When this result
was removed from the data set, the UAS gardeners’
BLL range was 0.6–4.1 lg dL-1 and the geometric
mean was 1.53 lg dL-1 (see Table 1 and Fig. 1).
There was no statistically significant difference
between the BLL of the UAS gardeners and those of
the non-gardening control group (p = 0.391). The
range of saliva Pb concentrations (mean of the two
saliva samples collected) was 1.1 to 4.5 lg L-1 with
geometric mean 2.4 lg dL-1 for the control group,
and the range was 1.3 to 7.9 lg L-1 with geometric
mean 3.12 lg L-1 for the UAS gardeners. The
difference in concentration between the first and
second saliva samples ranged from 0.005 to 6.2 lg

123

Environ Geochem Health (2022) 44:3493–3513

L-1 (24% to 376%) with median 1.2 lg L-1 (99%)
(Supplementary Information, Table SI1).
Environmental data
Nearly 280 soil samples were collected from 31 UAS
garden plots. Soil Pb concentrations for the three UAS
had a geometric mean of 327 mg kg-1 and a 95th
percentile of 680 mg kg-1. A summary of soil Pb
concentrations is presented in Fig. 2. Variability of
soil Pb concentrations between the three UAS was not
significant (p = 0.234); however, it was significant
between individual gardens at the same site, potentially demonstrating the effect of different gardening
practices both current and historic on Pb concentrations. Investigation of the drivers of this intra-site
variability is beyond the scope of this current paper
and as such is not considered further here. Home tap
water Pb concentrations ranged from 0.06 to 12.0 lg
L-1 (geometric mean 0.43 lg L-1). Where participants thought they had Pb pipes for domestic water
(17% of participants), the geometric mean Pb concentration was 3.49 lg L-1. 55% of participants did
not know whether they had Pb pipes in their home, and
the geometric mean for their domestic tap water Pb
was 0.24 lg L-1. 28% reported that they did not have
Pb pipes or solder in their home, and their home tap
water Pb concentrations had geometric mean 0.23 lg
L-1. The highest measured Pb concentration in tap
water (12.0 lg L-1, not flushed) was for a home where
occupants were aware of their Pb pipes and flushed out
standing water prior to the use of tap water for drinking
or cooking. A summary of domestic tap water Pb
concentrations is presented in Table 2, and the full data
set is available as Supplementary Information
Table SI2.
Questionnaire data
Of all the participants, 29% reported some form of
occupational or hobby exposure to Pb (Table 1). The

