Palm oil is one of the 17major oils and fats produced and traded
in the world today. Within the span of four decades, palm oil
has emerged as the fastest growing oil in the world. In fact, palm
oil is projected to be the world’s largest oil produced, although
it is currently occupying second position after soybean oil
(Bahruddin et alm,2013). Most palm oil is currently produced
in Southeast Asia, even though the oil palm is originally an
Africa crop, which was introduced to Southeast Asia in the 19th
century. The two largest producers are Malaysia and Indonesia,
who together account for roughly 85% of the world palm oil
production.
Palm oil, obtained from a tropical plant, Elaesis guineensis. The
genus Elaeis comprises two species, namely E. guineensis and
E. oleifera. Palm oil is a lipid extracted from the fleshly orange
red mesocarp of the fruits of the oil palm tree. The oil palm tree
has an unbranched stem and belongs to the the palmae family.

